---
import { site } from "../data/site";
import { trustPages } from "../data/trust-pages";
import TrustPageLayout from "../layouts/TrustPageLayout.astro";

const issueUrl = site.contact.url;
const page = trustPages.corrections;
---

<TrustPageLayout page={page} currentKey="corrections">
  <h2>Submit a report</h2>
  <p>
    Open a <a href={issueUrl}>public GitHub issue</a> with the article URL, the passage
    in question, and a supporting source when available.
  </p>
  <p>
    A GitHub account is required. A clear report identifies what is wrong and
    why it matters; a proposed wording change is welcome but not required.
  </p>
  <p class="notice">
    GitHub issues are public. Do not include private, confidential, financial,
    account, credential, or security-sensitive information.
  </p>
  <h2>Evaluation and response</h2>
  <p>
    Correction review compares the report with the cited and newly supplied
    evidence. It considers whether the issue changes a factual claim,
    recommendation, limitation, source interpretation, or practical outcome.
    Submitting a report does not guarantee that a requested change will be
    accepted.
  </p>
  <h2>Dates and correction notes</h2>
  <p>
    Articles retain their first-publication date. A substantive revision gets an
    updated date; a review date is used only when a review actually occurred.
    Dates are not changed merely to make a guide appear newer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Minor typographical, spelling, formatting, or link-label corrections may not
    receive a formal update note or modification date when they do not change
    meaning. A substantial factual correction should receive a transparent
    correction note on the affected article, together with an accurate
    modification or review date when applicable.
  </p>
  <p>
    An article without a formal correction note may still have received minor
    copy or formatting improvements.
  </p>
</TrustPageLayout>
